readings.c2s3:
{{smaller block|<poem>It is the part of men to fear and tremble,
When the most mighty gods, by tokens, send
Such dreadful heralds to ''astonish'' us.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', J. C., i. 3.}}}}
'''3.''' To strike or impress with wonder, surprise, or admiration; surprise; amaze.
{{smaller block|Thou hast ''astonish’d'' me with thy high terms.
{{right|''Shak.'', 1 Hen. VI., i. 2.}}
The student of Nature wonders the more and is ''astonished'' the less, the more conversant he becomes with her operations.
{{right|''Huxley'', Lay Sermons, p. 260.}}
What shall we say of the ocean telegraph, that extension of the eye and ear, whose sudden performance ''astonished'' mankind?
{{right|''Emerson'', Works and Days.}}}}
{{smaller|='''Syn. 3.''' ''Surprise'', ''Amaze'', etc. (see ''surprise''); startle, shock.}}

'''astonishable†''' (a̤-ston′ish-a̤-bl), ''a.'' [< ''astonish'' + ''-able''.] Astonishing.

'''astonishedly''' (a̤-ston′isht-li), ''adv.'' In an astonished manner. [Rare.]

'''astonisher''' (a̤-ston′ish-ėr), ''n.'' One who or that which astonishes.

'''astonishing''' (a̤-ston′ish-ing), ''p. a.'' Causing or fitted to cause astonishment; amazing; wonderful. {{smaller|='''Syn.''' Amazing, surprising, wonderful, marvelous.}}

'''astonishingly''' (a̤-ston′ish-ing-li), ''adv.'' In an astonishing manner; to an astonishing degree.

'''astonishingness''' (a̤-ston′ish-ing-nes), ''n.'' The quality of exciting astonishment. [Rare.]

'''astonishment''' (a̤-ston′ish-ment), ''n.'' [< ''astonish'' + ''-ment''. Cf. OF. ''estonissement'' (Palsgrave).] '''1.''' The state of being astonished. {{smaller|(''a''†) The state of being stunned or benumbed.}}

readings.c3s1:
{{smaller block|A coldness and ''astonishment'' in his loins, as folk say.
{{right|''Holland.''}}}}
(''b''†) Confusion of mind from sudden fear or other emotion; consternation.
{{smaller block|''Astonishment'' is that state of the soul in which all its motions are suspended with some degree of horror.
{{right|''Burke'', Sublime and Beautiful.}}}}
(''c''†) Passion; excitement; frenzy.
{{smaller block|<poem>Furious ever I knew thee to be,
Yet never in this strange ''astonishment''. ''Spenser.''</poem>}}
(''d'') Great surprise or wonder; amazement.
{{smaller block|We found, with no less wonder to us than ''astonishment'' to themselves, that they were the two valiant and famous brothers.
{{right|''Sir P. Sidney.''}}}}
'''2.''' A cause or matter of consternation.
{{smaller block|Thou shalt become an ''astonishment'', a proverb, and a byword among all nations.
{{right|Deut. xxviii. 37.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Those imaged, to the pride of kings and priests,
A dark yet mighty faith, a power as wide
As is the world it wasted, and are now
But an ''astonishment''.</poem>
{{right|''Shelley'', Prometheus Unbound, iii. 4.}}}}
='''Syn. 1.''' Amazement, admiration, awe.

'''astony''' (as-ton′i), ''v. t.''; pret. and pp. ''astonied'', ppr. ''astonying''. [< ME. ''astonien'', rarely ''astunien'': see ''aston''.] '''1†.''' To stun, as with a blow.
{{smaller block|The captain of the Helots . . . strake Palladias upon the side of his head that he reeled ''astonied''.
{{right|''Sir P. Sidney'', Arcadia, i. 23.}}}}
'''2.''' To astonish; terrify; confound. [Obsolete or archaic.]
{{smaller block|And when I heard this thing, I rent my garment and my mantle, . . . and sat down ''astonied''.
{{right|Ezra ix. 3.}}}}
{{smaller block|''Astonying'' with their suddenness both their friends and their enemies.
{{right|''Knolles.''}}}}
{{smaller block|And I ''astonied'' fell and could not pray. ''Mrs. Browning.''}}

readings.c3s2:
¶
'''astore†''', ''v. t.'' [< ME. ''astoren'' (and by apheresis ''storen'', > mod. E. ''store''), < OF. ''estorer'', ''estaurer'', < L. ''instaurare'', repair, renew: see ''instauration'' and ''store''.] To store; furnish with stores.
{{smaller block|Ful riche he was ''astored'' prively.
{{right|''Chaucer'', Gen. Prol. to C. T., l. 609.}}}}

'''Astoreth''' (as′tō-reth), ''n.'' [See ''Ashtoreth''.] Same as ''Astarte''.

'''astound†''' (a̤-stound′), ''p. a.'' [Early mod. E. also ''astown’d'', < ME. ''astouned'', ''astoned'', ''astuned'', pp. of ''astounen'', ''astonen'', ''astunen'', astonish: see ''aston'', ''astony'', and cf. ''astound'', ''v.''] Astonished; confounded. See ''aston''.
{{smaller block|<poem>The elf therewith ''astound''
Upstarted lightly.</poem>
{{right|''Spenser''.}}}}

'''astound''' (a̤-stound′), ''v.'' [As an inf. this form is late, being due in part to the pp. ''astound'', ''astouned'', and in part perhaps to the frequent dissimilated gemination of final ''-n'' into ''-nd'', as in ''sound'' for ''soun'', etc.; so dial. ''drownd'' for ''drown'', pp. ''drownded'' for ''drowned''.] '''I.''' ''trans.'' To astonish greatly; strike dumb with amazement; amaze; alarm.
{{smaller block|<poem>These thoughts may startle well, but not ''astound''
The virtuous mind.</poem>
{{right|''Milton'', Comus, l. 210.}}
In the architecture and embellishments of the chamber, the evident design had been to dazzle and ''astound''.
{{right|''Poe'', Tales, I. 375.}}}}
{{smaller|='''Syn.''' ''Surprise'', ''Astonish'', ''Amaze'', etc. (see ''surprise''); confound, stagger, dumfounder, stupefy, shock.}}

'''II.''' ''intrans.'' To cause astonishment; amaze; stun.
{{smaller block|<poem>The lightnings flash a larger curve, and more
The noise ''astounds''.</poem>
{{right|''Thomson'', Summer, l. 1138.}}}}
